= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

In <field characteristic> $p$, the <binomial theorem> gives $(X-b)^p=X^p-b^p$, since the intermediate <binomial coefficients> are divisible by the <prime number> $p$. If $b\in F$ is a <root> of $X^p-a$, then $b^p=a$, and hence
$$
\boxed{X^p-a=(X-b)^p\quad\text{in }F[X].}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Let $\alpha$ be a <root> in a <splitting field>. The preceding characteristic-$p$ identity gives $X^p-a=(X-\alpha)^p$, so there is only one distinct <root>. If the <polynomial> were reducible over $F$, choose a monic proper factor $g\in F[X]$ of <degree> $d$, with $1\leq d<p$. In the splitting field it must be $g=(X-\alpha)^d$. Its coefficient of $X^{d-1}$ is $-d\alpha$, which belongs to $F$. Since $d$ is nonzero and thus invertible in the <field> $F$, this forces $\alpha\in F$, contradicting the absence of a <root> there. Therefore **$X^p-a$ is irreducible over $F$**.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Suppose a <rational function> $P(t)/Q(t)\in\mathbb F_p(t)$, with nonzero <polynomials> $P,Q$, had $p$th power $t$. Then $P(t)^p=tQ(t)^p$, so comparison of <degrees> would give $p\deg P=1+p\deg Q$, impossible modulo $p$. Thus $X^p-t$ has no <root> in $K$, and part (b) proves it is <irreducible>.

In a <splitting field> containing $\alpha$ with $\alpha^p=t$,
$$
\boxed{X^p-t=(X-\alpha)^p.}
$$
It has one distinct <root> with multiplicity $p$, so it is inseparable. This is the <purely inseparable polynomial over a rational function field>.

= B
